.TH yPARSE· 5 2015-jun "linux" "end-user data files and data streams"

.SH NAME
.B yPARSE
- text file parsing and aggregating library

.SH PATRON
.B jupiter-terminus (boundaries)
god of boundaries and markers

.SH IMAGERY
head and torso of a man, connected to a boundary stone as a base

.SH SUMMARY
standardizes the reading/parsing and writing/aggregating of structured,
multi-type, ascii-text data streams and files required by most non-trivial
applications than dont require a binary database.

.SH DOCUMENTATION FILES
.nf  
yPARSE (3)          overall discussion
yPARSE_config (3)   general startup and shutdown
yPARSE_input (3)    structure of config, files, and streams
yPARSE_output (3)   structure of config, files, and streams
.B yPARSE (5) ´´´´´´´´ target data files and streams structure
yPARSE (7)          decision rationale, scope, and objectives

.SH TABLE OF CONTENTS
.nf  
A) purpose          focus of this man page
B) linux example    output from a linux command
C) conf example     system utility configuration file
D) app example      spreadsheet content data file
E) spider example   spider script example
F) turtle example   turtle graphics script (for spider)
G) ctags example    ctags function identification

.SH A) PURPOSE
yPARSE focuses exclusively on efficiently and reliably creating and parsing
8bit, ascii-text, columnar data files.  to help understand this library,
several examples of data files will been shown below and their purpose/structure
briefly described.

.SH B) LINUX EXAMPLE
the ps command, like so many unix/linux commands, is "mostly" columnar, but
really space delimited (one or more), except where is isn't (in the command
field) ;)
   -- columnar for ease of human understanding
   -- field separator is 0x20, the ascii space
   -- this version (ps -au) has ten (10) fields from left-to-right
   -- no "verb" is given as all lines, except the title are the same

.nf
